WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.986
Twenty deliveries on time, in both worlds. One home
gets nothing. The other

00:00:05.986 --> 00:00:08.748
gives every home at least three.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.640
The creator asks for twenty on time, three at every
home, and at

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:33.280
most ten turns of waiting. Muse Spark writes the
contract. The creator confirms

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:38.920
it. Ask for another courier, and Muse Spark refuses
the whole request. The

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:40.221
workers stay locked.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.873
Moving the lower stick cuts waiting from twenty-two
turns to ten. The total

00:00:59.873 --> 00:01:05.745
stays twenty of twenty-four. Four late deliveries
move onto another home. That home

00:01:05.745 --> 00:01:08.907
gets nothing on time. The brief fails.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.344
Select the late arrival. It reaches the home at turn
eighty-four, four turns

00:01:33.344 --> 00:01:38.688
late. The courier lights up on the map. The receipt
names the bridge

00:01:38.688 --> 00:01:40.333
and the simulator events.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.363
Move the lower bridge by one cell. Waiting breaks the
cap in one

00:02:09.363 --> 00:02:14.725
direction. On-time deliveries break it in the other.
The passing layout has a

00:02:14.725 --> 00:02:15.138
boundary.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:38.371
Save the project and open it again. The brief and the
measurements come

00:02:38.371 --> 00:02:44.741
back, recomputed. LiveWorld was built by Tang Vu.
Meta reads the request. The

00:02:44.741 --> 00:02:48.662
simulator decides. This demo uses a generated
tabletop.
